WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:05.033 position:58%
Tea in this area started around 1924.

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.474
In Kenya as a whole,

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:12.474
we cultivate maybe roughly
about 160,000 hectares of tea

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.852
which produces I'd say about
350 metric tonnes a year.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:21.721 position:56%
That's why we are one of the largest
tea producers globally.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.193
Second only to China,

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:31.564 position:66%
Kenya is a major tea producer
where it's a vital and lucrative business.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.671
and is one of the top
foreign exchange earners at 23%.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:43.109
About 5 million people directly
and indirectly are supported

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.477
by the crop in Kenya.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:49.477 position:61%
And for about 650,000 people,
it's the backbone of their livelihoods.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:52.752
But the country's dependence
on key foreign exports

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.221
hasn't always translated
into increased benefits

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:59.259 position:56%
for smallholder tea farmers,
which make up the bulk of producers.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:01.061
Most of the tea in Kenya

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:02.028
is smallholder farmers.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.197
To have a factory you
require a lot of tea.

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:07.267 position:59%
So for the smallholders who have small
ancestral pieces of property,

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.302
you need to take to tea
somewhere to be processed.

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:13.440
The small-scale farmers have
about 100,000 hectares of land

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.110
and they produce about 60%
of all the tea production.

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.712 position:63%
The rest of 40% is held in larger farms,

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.915 position:66%
which are owned now by the multinationals.

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.384 position:63%
Think Unilever, James Finlay Williamson,

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:25.652
and Kapchorua Tea.

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:29.389
Though they make up the majority
of tea grown and exported in Kenya,

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:32.826
there's a push to establish
Kenyan tea on the global stage

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:34.894
as its own brand and identity.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.297
What is picked exactly?
How does it work?

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:40.200 position:66%
You look for one, which has got two leaves
and a bud, something like that.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.234
That's what is picked.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:44.337 position:56%
And then it's thrown into the basket
as you see it being done.

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:48.441 position:61%
Usually it's done by ladies and you can
see it's a continuous process, right?

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.810
So you have to continue
picking it because it grows.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.178
And they are large workforce

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.080
because as you can see,
there's a lot to be picked

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.617 position:56%
and then it's put into their baskets
and then that is what is weighed

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.119 position:58%
and that's how they're paid for that.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.689
Then it's transported to
the factory and also weighed

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:07.127 position:56%
and sorted there in different grades
and then transformed into black tea.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:10.563
That goes directly to the auction,
where it's bought by buyers.

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:15.235
Those buyers are buying on behalf
of various packaging companies.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:18.605 position:61%
So that's how they pack it into Lipton,
turns into PJ Tips into all that.

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:21.107 position:58%
But we have a few local packers also.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:25.478
Since the war in Ukraine, auction
prices for tea have been volatile,

00:02:25.478 --> 00:02:29.082
and despite good weather helping
to increase higher production

00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:32.685 position:61%
export destinations saw sharp declines.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:36.122 position:56%
Former President Kenyatta emphasized
the need for farmers to increase

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:41.122 position:59%
value-addition to 90% of the tea grown
instead of selling it raw.

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:44.831 position:64%
We're not getting value as Kenyans
in this process and we need to get value.

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:48.034
We have to remove it
from the control of a few people.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:51.371
All right, currently like now
it is controlled by Unilever.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:54.641 position:58%
It was the one which was having
all of these brands across the globe.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:55.942
We want to be part of this.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:59.579
So we also want to be producing
and also ultimately

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:02.182
packaging our own and
marketing our own tea.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:06.686 position:63%
In that way, we as Kenyans, become major
stakeholders in the value chain.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:10.657
Once that happens, we can ensure
the growth of tea in the future.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:15.495 position:66%
Wainaina and Joseph are among the many
farmers in litigation against corporations

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:19.799 position:66%
like Unilever to maintain
a better position within the supply chain.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:21.901
Everybody is fighting
for their different rights.

00:03:21.901 --> 00:03:25.338 position:61%
Some of those in those farms
are fighting for historical injustices.

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:30.338 position:63%
They want those farms to be re-allocated
to the local communities.

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:34.214 position:64%
At the end of the day, all we are looking
for is fairness for the minorities,

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.082
so that we can all participate,

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:39.385 position:66%
and enjoy the value of our tea plantation.

